The '''Kingdom of Galwa (1822–1892)''' was the period of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|western Burawi kingdom]] following the [[Burawi War]] and the creation of [[Kijura]]. It was marked by postwar domestic crisis, reformist attempts to weaken the feudal order, gradual commercial liberalisation, limited industrial development, and a cautious re-approachment with the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. The era ended with the integration of the North Kaftian colony into the kingdom as the province of [[Laga]] in 1872.

Galwa entered the period as a predominantly agricultural state with only two major cities and a large rural population bound by feudal and tenant obligations. Although the [[Rights of Man Act (Galwa)]] of 1812 had formally abolished slavery and serfdom, much of the countryside remained organised around customary obligations and landlord power. In response, the crown pursued reforms intended to encourage urbanisation, expand commerce, and create a broader domestic market. The sale of land near cities, the weakening of feudal privilege, and the growth of port commerce gradually shifted the kingdom toward a limited industrial economy.

== Reign of Nuur VI (1822–c. 1840s) ==

{{main|Nuur VI of Galwa}}

[[Nuur VI]] inherited the postwar kingdom after the conclusion of the Burawi War. The destruction caused by the conflict convinced the court that Galwa could not preserve its independence while remaining a largely feudal agrarian kingdom. Nuur VI therefore adopted a reformist program intended to strengthen the crown, reduce aristocratic power, and encourage urban growth.

The first phase of reform centred on land and tenure. Royal land near the principal cities was sold to merchants and wealthy commoners, and some feudal obligations were relaxed or converted into monetary rents. The crown also encouraged settlement in and around the two major cities, where administration, trade, and craft production could be more closely supervised. These measures did not abolish the old landlord class, but they reduced its dominance and made urban property ownership more attractive to the emerging middle strata.

Nuur VI also treated urbanisation as a political necessity. He and his advisers believed that Galwa's limited number of cities and thin commercial base had made the kingdom vulnerable in war. By concentrating population and investment in the urban centres, the crown hoped to create a more tax-efficient economy, a larger market for manufactured goods, and a more reliable base for state service. In the same period, the crown expanded religious freedom cautiously, broadening protections for non-Catholic worship in the cities and reducing the ability of local landlords to enforce religious conformity. The reforms produced significant internal migration, as rural inhabitants moved toward the cities in search of wages, administrative employment, and access to expanding markets. The process did not eliminate feudalism, but it weakened landlord power and created a larger urban population linked to commerce and state service.

== Reign of Cali V (c. 1840s–1892) ==

{{main|Cali V of Galwa}}

[[Cali V]] continued the reformist course of his predecessor while placing greater emphasis on development and state capacity. His reign coincided with the gradual expansion of trade, the importation of technology, and the growth of limited industry in the principal towns. Rather than attempting to remake the kingdom through abrupt political change, Cali V used the crown's limited resources to encourage commerce and improve infrastructure.

=== Commercial liberalisation ===

In 1852, Galwa enacted the [[Free Trade Act of 1852]], which expanded access to port commerce and reduced restrictions on foreign exchange. The policy reflected the kingdom's position as a natural stopping point for shipping between [[Illypnia]] and the eastern coasts of [[Abyala]]. Free trade encouraged a mercantile boom in the port cities and increased the importance of customs revenues to the crown.

The kingdom maintained amicable relations with [[Breisland]] and the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]], both of which remained important external partners. Galwa's location and commercial policy made it a useful intermediary between Kaftian and Abyalan markets, and the crown used these relationships to support domestic development without becoming dependent on any single foreign power.

=== Commons and urban reform ===

The growth of urban merchant and professional classes created pressure for political reform. In 1865, the crown enacted the [[Commoners' Act of 1865]], creating a [[House of Commons of Galwa|House of Commons]] to give the emerging urban elite a formal role in government. The new chamber was intended to broaden support for the monarchy, absorb demands for reform, and channel urban influence into constitutional forms rather than street politics.

The act also reflected the gradual weakening of the old landed order. Urban elites, merchants, and professionals increasingly shaped the kingdom's political agenda, while the nobility remained influential but no longer monopolised state power. The arrangement did not create full democracy, but it marked a significant concession to liberal pressure.

=== Railways and economic integration ===

During the 1860s, Galwa became more closely integrated into the Kaftian economy. In the north, the kingdom constructed a railway linking the principal agricultural and mineral districts to the eastern frontier. In 1868, the [[Sago-Galwa Railway]] was completed, connecting [[Sago]] with the Galwa capital. The line improved the movement of agricultural produce and mineral resources and strengthened commercial exchange with Burawa.

The railways did not create full industrialisation, but they accelerated the emergence of a limited industrial sector. Ports, rail stations, and urban workshops became the main centres of modern economic activity, while the countryside remained dominated by tenant farming and customary obligations.

=== Education and administration ===

The reform era also expanded state involvement in education and administration. The crown promoted secular instruction and technical training in order to create a more capable bureaucracy and a workforce suited to urban and industrial life. This policy complemented earlier educational reforms and reduced the relative influence of clerical institutions.

== The Praga Affair and North Kaftia (1872) ==

In 1872, the North Kaftian colony was formally incorporated into the kingdom as the province of [[Laga]] following the [[Praga Affair]]. The affair began when the [[Royal Galwa North Kaftian Company]] sold territory to [[Nugal]], a transaction that created a minor political dispute with Burawa. After negotiations, the territory was restored to Galwa and reorganised as a full province.

The incorporation of Laga marked the culmination of the kingdom's colonial reorganisation. What had begun as a crown company possession became an integrated part of the state, reflecting the growing importance of the northern territories to Galwa's economy and administration.

== Legacy ==

The period from 1822 to 1892 marked Galwa's transition from a feudal postwar kingdom to a limited industrial and commercial state. The monarchy survived by pursuing reform, encouraging urban growth, expanding trade, and tolerating a gradual weakening of the old landed order. Although feudal relations persisted in the countryside, the kingdom's political economy had changed substantially by the end of the period.

The era also established the cautious external policy that characterised late nineteenth-century Galwa. The kingdom remained wary of Burawa, but re-approachment became possible through trade, rail connections, and practical cooperation. Galwa preserved its monarchy while adapting to the pressures of a more commercial and industrial age.

